course: language
lesson: 41
title: Alice's word games: puns, parodies and nonsense
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: «Alice's Adventures in Wonderland» is a book you can read twice: once for the story, and once for the word games hidden in nearly every page.
explain: The Mouse says «Mine is a long and a sad tale!» and Alice, looking at its tail, says «It is a long tail, certainly, but why do you call it sad?» That is a pun: two words that sound the same, «tale» and «tail», and the joke is that each speaker means a different one.
explain: The Mock Turtle's school subjects are puns too: Reeling and Writhing for reading and writing, and four kinds of sums: Ambition, Distraction, Uglification and Derision, for addition, subtraction, multiplication and division.
explain: Many of the poems Alice recites come out wrong, as parodies: the tune and shape of a famous school poem with silly new words, like «Twinkle, twinkle, little bat! How I wonder what you're at!»
explain: Some lines are pure nonsense with a sharp point inside: the Hatter's riddle «Why is a raven like a writing-desk?» has no answer at all, and «Curiouser and curiouser!» is bad grammar Alice uses because she is so surprised.
fact: A pun is a joke built on a word with two meanings or on two words that sound alike, like «tale» (a story) and «tail» (the end of an animal).
fact: The Mock Turtle's teacher was called Tortoise «because he taught us», and the school's lessons got shorter each day «because they lessen from day to day».
fact: The Mock Turtle's subjects are sound-alike jokes: Mystery (history), Seaography (geography), Drawling (drawing), Stretching (sketching), Fainting in Coils (painting in oils), and Laughing and Grief (Latin and Greek).
fact: The Mock Turtle says no wise fish would go anywhere without a «porpoise», which sounds like «purpose».
fact: The Mouse calls its history «the driest thing I know»: dry means both «not wet» and «boring», and the soaked animals need the first kind.
fact: A parody copies the shape and rhythm of a well-known poem or song but changes the words to make it funny.
fact: «How doth the little crocodile» is a parody of Isaac Watts's poem «How doth the little busy bee», and «Twinkle, twinkle, little bat» is a parody of «Twinkle, twinkle, little star».
fact: «You are old, Father William» is a parody of a serious poem by Robert Southey, in which an old man gives wise advice; in Carroll's version the old man stands on his head and turns back-somersaults.
fact: The Duchess twists the proverb «Take care of the pence and the pounds will take care of themselves» into «Take care of the sense, and the sounds will take care of themselves».
fact: The Hatter's riddle «Why is a raven like a writing-desk?» has no answer; Carroll admitted it, though later he and his readers invented answers for fun.
fact: «Curiouser and curiouser» is not proper English; the right way to say it is «more and more curious», and the book itself says Alice «quite forgot how to speak good English».
fact: Alice mixes up «antipodes» (the people on the opposite side of the Earth) with «antipathies» (strong dislikes), a funny word mistake called a malapropism.
fact: The March Hare and the Hatter show that changing the order of words changes the meaning: «I say what I mean» is not the same as «I mean what I say», just as «I eat what I see» is not «I see what I eat».
exercise: What is a pun? | A joke on a word with two meanings or a sound-alike | A short poem with exactly three lines and a pause in it | A long story that teaches you a lesson at the end | A word that means the opposite of another word
exercise: Which two words does the Mouse's «long and sad tale» play on? | Tale and tail | Long and song | Sad and said | Mine and mouse
exercise: Why was the teacher called Tortoise? | Because he taught us | Because he was very slow | Because he lived in a shell | Because he was a hundred years old
exercise: Why are they called «lessons», according to the Gryphon? | Because they lessen from day to day | Because the teachers say less and less | Because there are less of them than games | Because each one lasts a little less than an hour
exercise: What real subjects are «Reeling and Writhing»? | Reading and writing | Rowing and riding | Rest and running | Rowing and racing
exercise: Which sum is hidden inside «Uglification»? | Multiplication | Division | Addition | Subtraction
exercise: What subject is «Seaography»? | Geography | Biology | History | Drawing
exercise: What does «Fainting in Coils» stand for? | Painting in oils | Falling in the coal | Fencing in castles | Fishing in rivers
exercise: What does «porpoise» sound like in the Mock Turtle's joke? | Purpose | Pork | Porch | Purse
exercise: What two meanings of «dry» make the Mouse's history lesson funny? | Not wet, and boring | Thirsty, and sunny | Old, and dusty | Short, and quiet
exercise: What is a parody? | A copy of a famous poem's shape, with funny new words | A list of the hard words at the back of a book | A true story about a writer's own life | A poem that must always be sung aloud by a whole group
exercise: Which poem is «Twinkle, twinkle, little bat» a parody of? | Twinkle, twinkle, little star | Humpty Dumpty sat on a wall | Baa, baa, black sheep | Hey diddle diddle, the cat and the fiddle
exercise: Which animal replaced the busy bee in Alice's version of Watts's poem? | A crocodile | A lobster | A snail | A whiting
exercise: What is the answer to «Why is a raven like a writing-desk?» in the book? | There is none at all | Because both have feathers | Because both have legs | Because both are black
exercise: What should Alice have said instead of «curiouser and curiouser»? | More and more curious | Curious and curiouser | Most curiousest of all | Curiously and curiouser
exercise: What did Alice mean when she said «the Antipathies»? | The antipodes, people on the other side of the Earth | People who hate each other because they live far away | A country in the middle of Africa with a funny name | The animals she met at the bottom of the rabbit hole
exercise: Which proverb did the Duchess twist into «Take care of the sense»? | Take care of the pence | Look before you leap | Haste makes waste | Waste not, want not
test: «the old crab taught Laughing and Grief lol what even is that» What is the joke? | It sounds like Latin and Greek, two subjects old schools taught | It means he laughed in the mornings and cried in the afternoons | It is a real subject taught in schools under the sea | It is a mistake by the printer that nobody ever fixed
test: Your teacher writes «Ambition, Distraction, Uglification, Derision» on the board and asks what they are. What do you say? | The four sums in sound-alike form: addition, subtraction, multiplication, division | Four bad habits that the Mock Turtle wanted children to avoid | The names of the four houses at the Mock Turtle's school | Four old words for being proud, bored, ugly and rude, written on old classroom walls
test: «the lessons got shorter every day, so on day 11 they had none, right?» Why does the Gryphon say they «lessen»? | It's a pun: «lessons» sounds like «lessen», which means get smaller | Because the teachers got lazier each day of the week | Because the Mock Turtle forgot a bit more every night | Because the sea washed one page of the lesson book away each day, until nothing was left
test: A friend writes a poem: «Hickory dickory dog, the mouse ran up the frog». What has she written? | A parody of a famous nursery rhyme | A pun on two words that sound the same | A riddle that has no answer | A malapropism about the antipodes
test: Why is «You are old, Father William» funnier if you know Southey's poem? | The serious old man becomes a silly one who stands on his head | Southey's poem is about a mouse who tells a sad tale | Southey's poem was written in old French and nobody in England could understand a word of it | It is not funnier: the two poems are exactly the same
test: The Hatter's riddle has no answer. Why might Carroll have written a riddle like that? | To show that Wonderland talk often sounds clever but means nothing | Because he forgot the answer while he was writing the book and never found his notes again | Because the answer was a secret only Alice Liddell knew | Because riddles were against the law in Victorian England
test: A boy says «I'm gooder at football than him». Like whom in the book has he forgotten good English? | Alice, saying «curiouser and curiouser» | The Mock Turtle, saying «porpoise» | The Mouse, saying «the driest thing I know» | The Hatter, saying «writing-desk»
test: «my nan said she's going to the antipathies for Christmas» What does Nan mean, and what slip did she make? | She means the antipodes, the far side of the Earth, and swapped it for a sound-alike word | She means she will spend Christmas with people she dislikes and said it correctly | She means a place in Scotland called the Antipathies, and spelled it right | She means a Christmas party game and used the old Victorian word for it, which is still correct today
test: Is «I eat what I see» the same as «I see what I eat»? | No: the first means you eat everything you see | Yes: the same words always mean the same thing | Yes: both mean you look at your food | No: the second means you eat everything you see
test: Which of these lines from the book is NOT a pun? | «Off with her head!» | «Because he taught us» | «They lessen from day to day» | «A long tail, certainly»
test: ★★★ Using the Mock Turtle's code (Reeling = reading, Drawling = drawing, Mystery = history), what would «Fizzicks and Kemistree» probably mean? | Physics and chemistry | Fishing and keeping trees | Fizzy drinks and chimneys | Fixing and kitchens
test: ★★★ Change the order of words and the Hatter's point shows: which pair means two DIFFERENT things? | «I like what I get» and «I get what I like» | «I am happy and tired» and «I am tired and happy» | «She ran fast» and «Fast she ran» | «Red and blue» and «blue and red»
exam: Identify the device employed by the author in the line «because he taught us», given in explanation of the name Tortoise. | A pun founded upon two expressions of similar sound | A parody of a well-known instructional poem | A riddle constructed so as to admit no answer whatsoever | A proverb altered for the purpose of satire
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a parody? | A composition imitating the form of a known work, with altered and humorous content | A composition in which every line rhymes with the line immediately preceding it, as in a nursery song | A factual account of the life of the author of a known work | A translation of a known work into a foreign language
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the riddle propounded by the Hatter. | It was devised without any answer, as the author subsequently acknowledged | Its answer is supplied by the Dormouse at the close of the chapter | Its answer is revealed by the Queen of Hearts during the trial of the Knave | Its answer appears upon the final page of the volume
exam: Ascertain the expression of which «Curiouser and curiouser» constitutes an ungrammatical rendering. | More and more curious | Most curious of all | Curious and more curious still | Less and less curious
